NAME
====

**wattle-applications** - how Wattle applications are laid out and installed

DESCRIPTION
===========

An application is a project that `wattle-apps(1)` installs. This page
describes what an application contains beyond what any project contains, and
where its files are installed. The info file, the exes, libs and docs and the
dependencies of a project are described in `wattle-projects(7)`, and
`wattle-build(1)` builds the exes and libs that an application declares.

LAYOUT
======

An application may contain these files:

- ``info.edn``:
  The info file, at the root of the application. Installing needs its ``:name``
  and ``:group``. See ``INFO FILE`` in `wattle-projects(7)`.

- ``hooks/init.wattle`` or ``hooks.wattle``:
  The application script. If both exist, ``hooks/init.wattle`` is used. The
  rest of ``hooks`` is installed with it, so the script can import the files
  beside it.

An application needs an application script or an info file that declares
``:exes``.

INSTALLATION
============

An application with an application script is installed by running the hooks
that the script defines, and nothing else. An application without one is
installed from its info file. Each exe of ``:type`` ``:native`` or ``:source``
is installed, with the libs and docs whose ``:for`` names it:

- A ``:native`` exe is built with `wattle-build(1)` and its program is
  installed in ``bin``. A native lib for it is linked into the program, and a
  source lib for it is compiled into the program.

- A ``:source`` exe is installed as described in `<Source programs>`.

- Each file of a ``:man`` doc is installed in ``share/man``, in the section
  that the digit at the end of its name gives.

A ``:remote`` lib is fetched under ``lib/wattle/.fetch`` and has the form its
own info file declares. The manifest records the ``:name``, ``:url`` and
``:rev`` of each under ``:libs``.

A ``:web`` exe is not installed. A lib or doc whose ``:for`` names no installed
exe is not installed, and a ``:web`` doc whose ``:for`` names an installed exe
is refused. The manifest lists each file installed in ``bin`` and
``share/man``, so ``wattle apps remove`` removes them with the application
directory.

Source programs
---------------

A ``:source`` exe is a program that is installed as source. Its ``:files``
are copied into the application directory. A directory is copied without any
entry named ``zig-out``, ``.zig-cache``, ``.wattle`` or ``.git``. A source lib
for it is copied to ``.wattle/<name>/`` there. A native lib for it is built
with `wattle-build(1)` in the project, and the native module is copied to
``zig-out/lib`` in the application directory. This needs **zig** on the
``PATH``, and ``wattle apps upgrade`` builds the modules again.

The program in ``bin`` has the name of the exe and starts with
``#!/usr/bin/env wattle``. It loads ``:entry`` and calls ``main`` with the path
of the program followed by the arguments. A bare ``import`` in the program
searches ``.wattle`` and ``zig-out/lib`` in the application directory, and a
vendored copy under ``deps`` is imported by a relative path. Running the
program needs **wattle** on the ``PATH`` and the prefix set with
``WATTLE_PREFIX``. A program that needs neither is a ``:native`` exe. Windows
has no shebang lines, so a ``:source`` exe cannot be installed there.

Example:

````
{:name "greeter"
 :group "net.inqk"
 :exes [{:type :source
         :name "greet"
         :entry "main.wattle"
         :files ["main.wattle" "lib" "deps"]}]
 :libs [{:type :source
         :name "util"
         :files ["util/"]
         :for ["greet"]}]
 :docs [{:type :man
         :files ["man/greet.1"]
         :for ["greet"]}]}
````

Hooks
-----

The application script defines a function for each hook it uses. Each takes the
application's manifest. The hooks run in this order at installation:

1. ``clean``, only when a clean build is requested
2. ``build``
3. ``install``
4. ``check``, only when a check is requested

The ``remove`` hook runs when the application is removed.

Within a hook, these functions add files to the manifest, so that
``wattle apps remove`` can remove them:

- ``app/add-bin``:
  Adds a program to the binpath.

- ``app/add-manpage``:
  Adds a man page to the manpath.

Example of ``hooks/init.wattle``:

````
(defn install
  [manifest &]
  (app/add-bin manifest "hello")
  (app/add-manpage manifest "hello.1"))
````

INSTALLED FILES
===============

All locations are under ``*prefix*``; see ``WATTLE_PREFIX`` in `wattle(1)`.

- ``lib/wattle``:
  The libpath, which holds the applications. ``import`` does not search it.
  See ``MODULES`` in `wattle(7)`.

- ``lib/wattle/apps/<group>/<name>``:
  The directory of an installed application: ``manifest.edn``, which lists its
  files and records its source, ``:rev``, the revision of the source, and the
  revision of each remote lib, a copy
  of its info file and a copy of its application script, with the rest of its
  ``hooks`` directory. The revision is the commit hash for a Git repository, and
  ``sha256:`` and a hash of the files for a tarball or a directory.

- ``bin``:
  The binpath, for programs.

- ``share/man``:
  The manpath, for man pages.

